      "note": "Pokorny's lemma on the very page Nourai cites is 'meit(h)-1, Meaning: staying (place)', and its whole Iranian material is exactly what Nourai puts in the Avestan box (maēθanam 'dwelling, house', miθnaiti 'stays, dwells'). The language label 'Indo-European' and the gloss 'to stay' are therefore both right, and the double citation form 'Meit, Meith' reproduces Pokorny's own bracketed '(h)'. Two caveats that do not affect the verdict: (a) Pokorny prints a second, homophonous lemma meit(h)-2 'to exchange, change' on the same p.715, and modern handbooks (Wiktionary's *meytH-, Edelman EDIV V s.v. *(1)mait-) increasingly treat the 'dwell' and 'exchange' groups as one root, so Wiktionary reaches Persian mehmān through *meytH- 'to change, exchange'; (b) outside Iranian and Baltic the root has almost no material, so it is a small, well-attested but narrowly based reconstruction. Downstream, the Persian box is fully sound; the only soft spot on this chart is that Avestan maēθman- is a reconstruction, not an attested word (see node #1).",
