          "derivation": "Two of the three forms are solidly Avestan, the third is a reconstruction. (1) 'maĕt' = the root maēθ- 'to dwell, stay', whose attested present is miθnaiti 'he dwells, stays' (nasal present *mit-na-, zero grade of maēθ-); Pokorny 715 quotes exactly this form. (2) 'maĕthanam' = Av. maēθana- n. 'dwelling, abode, station', cited in the accusative/nominative singular maēθanəm/maēθanam, which is how it appears throughout Yasna, the Yašts and the Vendīdād; Pokorny glosses it 'residence for people and gods, dwelling, house', and the Pahlavi Zand renders it māyān/mān 'house, home'. Nourai's gloss 'staying place, house, homeland' adds the later Persian 'homeland' sense, which belongs to New Persian mihan rather than to the Avestan word. (3) 'maĕthman' 'guest' is NOT attested in the extant Avesta: Hübschmann (Persische Studien pp.141–142) reconstructs it with an asterisk, 'mihmān \"Gast\" aus mehmān ... zd. *maiθman-', and in his sound-law list writes 'Ap. θm = np. hm: mihmān \"Gast\" = ap. *maiθman-'. Nourai prints it without an asterisk, as though attested; the derivation itself (a -man- stem from maēθ-, literally 'one who stays') is the standard one and is repeated by Mo'in. Hence 'plausible': the arrow root→Avestan is right, but one of the three Avestan words is a starred form.",
