          "derivation": "Both words show the Iranian cluster θ + nasal turning into h + nasal in Middle Persian, the sound law Hübschmann formulated as 'Old Iranian θm = New Persian hm'. (1) Old Iranian *maiθman- 'one who stays, lodger' > MP mēhmān, written m(ʾ)hmʾn', glossed by MacKenzie 'inhabitant, guest; acceptable' > NP mehmān مهمان; the by-form mihmān/mīhmān میهمان arises by the ordinary raising of pretonic ē > i before the following long syllable (Hübschmann: 'mihmān aus mehmān'), and the spelling with yāʾ is the one Ferdowsi-era and later poets use, as in the couplet Nourai quotes. Cognates confirm the shape: Pashto melma 'guest' (pl. melmā), Sarikoli mehmūn, Baluchi, Kurdish mêwan/mîwan, Awromani mēhmān. (2) Old Iranian maēθana- 'dwelling' > MP mēhan (MacKenzie 'home') > NP mihan میهن 'home, homeland, birthplace'; the same θn > hn is fossilised in the Khorasani place-names Mihana, Kešmihan (< *kaš-maēθana-) and Xarmihan. Mo'in adds that the Pahlavi Zand of Vendīdād 8 renders Avestan maēθana- by mān ud xānag, and that the Avesta manuscript variants actually write 'mihan ud xānag'. The semantic step 'dwelling' → 'homeland' is Persian and post-Islamic; the older dictionaries (Sorūri, Borhān, Anjoman-ārā) still gloss mihan only 'house and home, resting place, birthplace'.",
