          "derivation": "The two elements are exactly right, the compound as printed is Nourai's own analytic form. μέλας, gen. μέλανος 'black' continues *mel-n-, the n-stem of PIE *mel- 'dark' (Pokorny 720); χολή 'bile, gall' belongs to PIE *ǵʰel-/*ghel- 'to shine; yellow, green' — precisely Nourai's cross-reference ☞Ghel, and precisely Watkins's double etymology 'MELANCHOLY ghel-2, mel-2'. The compound actually attested in Greek, however, is not *μελαγχολή but μελαγχολία (Hippocratic corpus, 5th–4th c. BCE, in the humoral theory of the four fluids), beside the adjective μελάγχολος and the verb μελαγχολάω; the neuter μέλαινα χολή 'black bile' is the underlying phrase. So 'melan-xolĕ' should be read as Nourai's hyphenated analysis 'melan- + kholē', not as a citable Greek lemma; the gloss 'black bile, mental disorder' correctly gives both the literal and the medical sense, the second having arisen because an excess of the black humour was held to cause despondency and madness.",
